---
title: "Tutorial 4: Create a mammal voucher tag"
sidebar:
  label: "4. Create a voucher tag"
  order: 4
---

In this tutorial you design a two-sided voucher tag for mammal specimens and
print it from your NAHPU records. The tag follows the LSUMZ mammal label: the
front carries the locality, coordinates, preparation date, collector, field
number, and standard measurements; the back carries the species name, the
prepared parts, and a QR code that links the tag to its record.

You build two things:

- a **template**, which describes one tag, 100 × 34 mm;
- a **print layout**, which places 12 tags on a US Letter sheet, front and
  back.

{% aside type="note" %}
NAHPU ships a finished version of this tag, `Mammal voucher label`, with the
`Mammal voucher labels` print layout. Load it from `Setup NAHPU` or
`Load defaults`. If you have already loaded it, give your template another
name, such as `Mammal voucher tag (mine)`, and compare the two.
{% /aside %}

## Before you start

You need:

- a NAHPU project with a few complete mammal specimen records. For a useful
  preview, include a specimen with a long locality, one with several parts,
  and one with a measurement flagged as imprecise in its accuracy note;
- a printer that prints on both sides, and the card stock you use for tags;
- about 45 minutes.

## 1. Plan the tag

Sketch the tag before you open the editor. Each line on the tag becomes one
text element.

| Side | Element | NAHPU source | How it prints |
|---|---|---|---|
| Front | Locality | `geography::country`, `stateProvince`, `county`, `municipality`, `locality` | `Indonesia: Sumatera Selatan; Pagar Alam, Dempo Selatan, Kance Diwe, Bandar Jaya, Gunung Patah` |
| Front | Coordinates | `coordinate::decimalLatitude`, `decimalLongitude` | `-4.20857, 103.31445` |
| Front | Measurements | `mammalAttribute::totalLength`, `tailLength`, `hindFootLength`, `earLength`, `weight` | `359-167-41-24≡216.7g` |
| Front | Preparation date | `specimen::prepDate` | `August 30, 2023` |
| Front | Cataloger and field number | `personnel::name`, `specimen::fieldNumber` | `Dewi Silvestris 2116` |
| Back | Species | `taxonomy::genus`, `specificEpithet` | *Maxomys inflatus* |
| Back | Parts | `specimenPart::type` | `Skin, Skull, Skeleton, Liver, Lung, Cecum, Stomach` |
| Back | QR code | `specimen::uuid` | A scannable link to the record |

A thin vertical line on each side marks the margin where the string passes
through the tag.

## 2. Create the template

{% screenshots
   large="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-create-large.webp"
   largeAlt="NAHPU Create new template dialog on a tablet, naming the Mammal voucher tag"
   small="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-create-small.webp"
   smallAlt="NAHPU Create new template dialog on a phone" %}
{% pin id="A" label="Template name" large=[49.9, 34.7] small=[50, 20.7] /%}
{% pin id="B" label="Record type" large=[49.9, 42.3] small=[50, 27.6] /%}
{% pin id="C" label="Sides" large=[54.3, 51.9] small=[50, 36.4] /%}
{% pin id="D" label="Create" large=[56, 72.5] small=[67.4, 54.2] /%}
{% /screenshots %}


{% steps %}

1. Open `Settings` > `Template Editor`, or `Templates` under the dashboard's
   `Tools`, and select the add icon or `Create new` in the options menu.
2. Enter `Mammal voucher tag` as the `Template name` {% ref id="A" /%}. Add a short description,
   such as `Two-sided LSUMZ-style mammal tag`.
3. Set the `Record type` {% ref id="B" /%} to `Specimen`.
4. Under `Sides` {% ref id="C" /%}, choose `2 sided`. NAHPU creates separate
   Front and Back designs. Select `Create` {% ref id="D" /%}.
5. Set the template size to 100 mm wide and 34 mm high. This is the size of
   one tag, not of the sheet of paper. For other tag sizes, the
   `Template Size Preset` list includes `Vertebrate Specimen Tag (Medium)`.
6. Select `Save template` once now, so later edits have a saved starting
   point.

{% /steps %}

Before adding fields, select `Select specimen for text preview` in the toolbar
and choose one of your specimens. Text elements then show that record's values
as you work, instead of the raw placeholders.

## 3. Design the front

{% screenshots
   large="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-front-large.webp"
   largeAlt="NAHPU Template Editor on a tablet showing the front of the mammal voucher tag filled from a Gunung Patah specimen"
   small="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-front-small.webp"
   smallAlt="NAHPU Template Editor on a phone showing the front of the mammal voucher tag" %}
{% pin id="A" label="Add text" large=[28.7, 22.1] small=[7.6, 21.8] /%}
{% pin id="B" label="Add line" large=[37.3, 22.1] small=[33.3, 21.8] /%}
{% pin id="C" label="Locality text" large=[57.9, 43.8] small=[54.3, 54] /%}
{% pin id="D" label="Front and Back switch" large=[4.1, 93.5] small=[19.6, 92.5] /%}
{% /screenshots %}


Make sure `Front` is selected in the switch below the canvas {% ref id="D" /%}.
Add each element with `Add text` {% ref id="A" /%}, type or insert its content, then drag it into place. Use the grid
and snapping to keep the left edges aligned.

### Locality

{% steps %}

1. Select `Add text` and open the new element's text input.
2. Select `Insert Field`. Set the taxon filter to mammals, then insert the
   geography fields and type the separators between them:

   ```text
   [geography::country]: [geography::stateProvince]; [geography::county]; [geography::municipality], [geography::locality]
   ```

   Not every locality has a county. To avoid printing `; ;` when it is empty,
   make the county and its separator conditional with `Conditional output`:

   ```text
   [geography::country]: [geography::stateProvince]; [[if][geography::county!=""]=>"[geography::county]; "]][geography::municipality], [geography::locality]
   ```

3. Set the font size to 8 pt.
4. Set `Max Width (mm)` to about 70 so long localities wrap instead of running
   off the tag, and `Max Height (mm)` to about 12 to reserve three lines.
5. Place the element at the top of the tag, about 25 mm from the left edge.

{% /steps %}

Use the full `table::field` keys that `Insert Field` writes. A short key such
as `[locality]` can pick the wrong field when two tables share a name.

### Coordinates

Add a second text element below the locality, at 8 pt:

```text
[coordinate::decimalLatitude], [coordinate::decimalLongitude]
```

NAHPU uses the specimen's own coordinate. When the specimen has none and its
site has exactly one coordinate, NAHPU uses the site's.

### Measurements

Standard mammal measurements print in a fixed order: total length, tail,
hind foot, ear, and weight, with the three-line `≡` sign before the weight. A
measurement noted as imprecise in the specimen's accuracy note prints in
brackets, such as `[9.5]`.

{% steps %}

1. Add a text element and insert the five measurement fields with their
   separators:

   ```text
   [mammalAttribute::totalLength]-[mammalAttribute::tailLength]-[mammalAttribute::hindFootLength]-[mammalAttribute::earLength]≡[mammalAttribute::weight]g
   ```

   Insert the `≡` sign from `Available Symbols` in the `Text Input` dialog,
   which also lists the fields you can insert.

2. Select a measurement, open `Conditional output`, and choose
   `Conditional brackets`. Set the condition field to
   `mammalAttribute::accuracy`. NAHPU fills in the matching measurement for
   you.
3. Repeat for each of the five measurements. The text now reads:

   ```text
   [[mammalAttribute::totalLength][mammalAttribute::accuracy~="totalLength"]]-[[mammalAttribute::tailLength][mammalAttribute::accuracy~="tailLength"]]-[[mammalAttribute::hindFootLength][mammalAttribute::accuracy~="hindFootLength"]]-[[mammalAttribute::earLength][mammalAttribute::accuracy~="earLength"]]≡[[mammalAttribute::weight][mammalAttribute::accuracy~="weight"]]g
   ```

4. Align the element to the right with `Align right` and place it in the lower
   right of the tag.

{% /steps %}

On `mammalAttribute::accuracy`, `~=` asks whether that measurement is flagged
as imprecise. See
[Conditional output](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#conditional-output)
for the full syntax.

### Preparation date and cataloger

{% steps %}

1. Add a text element with `[specimen::prepDate]`. Under `Text type`, choose
   `Dates`, and under `Format`, choose `Month DD, YYYY`. Set the size to
   8.5 pt and place it in the lower left.
2. Add a text element with `[personnel::name] [specimen::fieldNumber]`. Align
   it right and place it in the lower right, below the measurements. On a
   specimen template, `personnel` fields are the specimen's cataloger.

{% /steps %}

Dates are stored as `yyyy-mm-dd`, so choose the format here rather than
typing the date out.

### String margin

Select `Add line` {% ref id="B" /%}, rotate it 90°, and place it about 20 mm from the left edge,
across the height of the tag. The strip to its left stays clear for the string
hole; keep the text elements to the right of the line.

## 4. Design the back

{% screenshots
   large="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-back-large.webp"
   largeAlt="NAHPU Template Editor on a tablet showing the back of the mammal voucher tag"
   small="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-back-small.webp"
   smallAlt="NAHPU Template Editor on a phone showing the back of the mammal voucher tag" %}
{% pin id="A" label="Species" large=[54.8, 59.1] small=[52.8, 57.7] /%}
{% pin id="B" label="Parts" large=[56.2, 74.7] small=[56.5, 62.4] /%}
{% pin id="C" label="QR code" large=[90.4, 46.5] small=[87.9, 53.7] /%}
{% pin id="D" label="Front and Back switch" large=[9.8, 93.5] small=[28, 92.4] /%}
{% /screenshots %}


Select `Back` in the switch below the canvas {% ref id="D" /%}.

{% steps %}

1. **Species** {% ref id="A" /%}. Add a text element with
   `[taxonomy::genus] [taxonomy::specificEpithet]`. Turn on `Italic`, set the
   size to 10 pt, and choose a serif font, such as `DejaVuSerif`. Place it in
   the middle of the tag.
2. **Parts** {% ref id="B" /%}. Add a text element with `[specimenPart::type]`. Under
   `Text type`, choose `List Values`, and under `Format`, choose
   `Comma separated (A, B, C)`. Set the size to 7 pt and `Max Width (mm)` to
   about 70, and place it along the bottom.
3. **QR code** {% ref id="C" /%}. Add a text element with `[specimen::uuid]` and turn on
   `QR Code`. Set `QR Size (mm)` to fit the top right corner, about 12 mm.
4. **String margin.** Add the same vertical line about 20 mm from the left
   edge, so it lines up with the front.

{% /steps %}

The QR code stores the specimen's UUID, which stays the same when the field
number or catalog number changes, so the tag always points to one record.

{% aside type="caution" %}
When a printer flips the sheet along its short edge, the back prints upside
down. If your test print shows this, turn on the Back side's 180° rotation in
the template editor and print the test sheet again.
{% /aside %}

## 5. Check the template with real records

Select `Select specimen for text preview` and switch between your test
specimens. Check that:

- a long locality wraps within its box and does not overlap the coordinates;
- a flagged measurement prints in brackets, and the others do not;
- a specimen with several parts lists them all on the back;
- a missing value leaves a clean gap. To print `N/A` instead, set
  `No Content Placeholder` on that element.

Select `Template border` to show a dashed cutting outline around each tag,
then select `Save template`.

## 6. Create the print layout

The template describes one tag. The print layout places tags on the sheet.

{% steps %}

1. Open `Settings` > `Documents` and switch to `Print layouts`.
2. Create a new layout named `Mammal voucher tags` and add a description,
   such as `12 two-sided tags per Letter sheet`.
3. Under `Page Setup`, choose `US Letter (8.5 x 11 in)` and `Portrait`. Set
   the page padding to 8 mm at the top and bottom and 4 mm at the left and
   right.
4. Under `Template blocks`, set the block's `Template` to `Mammal voucher tag`.
5. Set `Rows` to 6, `Cols` to 2, and `Copies` to 1. Keep `Auto-fill page` off:
   tags need a fixed grid.
6. Set `Template padding (mm)` to 1 on each side, so neighboring tags do not
   touch.
7. Set `Order by` to the specimen's field number and `Direction` to
   `Ascending`, so the tags come out in the order you file them.

{% /steps %}

Two 100 mm tags with their padding need 212 mm, which fits the 216 mm width
of a Letter sheet; six 34 mm rows fit its height with room to spare. A4 is
210 mm wide, so on A4 paper reduce the page padding or the tag width.

## 7. Export and print

{% screenshots
   large="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-export-large.webp"
   largeAlt="NAHPU Export documents screen on a tablet previewing six voucher tags on a Letter sheet"
   small="app-screenshots/tutorials/voucher-export-small.webp"
   smallAlt="NAHPU Export documents preview on a phone showing voucher tag fronts and backs" %}
{% pin id="A" label="Preview" large=[74.9, 48] small=[50, 34] /%}
{% pin id="B" label="Update preview" large=[89.8, 17.7] small=[72.4, 24.5] /%}
{% pin id="C" label="Export documents" large=[49.9, 93.3] small=[50, 92.2] /%}
{% /screenshots %}


{% steps %}

1. Open the project menu and select `Export documents`.
2. Set `Layout profile` to `Mammal voucher tags`.
3. Turn on `Override template blocks`. Under the block's
   `Specimens Selection`, select `Select`, then `Clear all`, and check the
   specimens to print. Without a selection, every specimen in the project
   prints.
4. Select `Generate preview` {% ref id="B" /%}; on a phone, open the preview
   tab first. Check the first and last pages, both sides, and
   the tag with the longest locality.
5. Enter a `File name`, such as `patah-voucher-tags`, and select
   `Export documents` {% ref id="C" /%}.
6. Print one sheet on plain paper, on both sides. Hold it up to the light and
   check that each back lines up with its front.
7. Print the full set on your tag stock, then cut along the outlines.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="tip" %}
Print a test sheet whenever you change the printer, the paper, or the layout.
The [printing equipment](/en/usages/export/export-documents/#printing-equipment)
notes describe a printer and archival paper the NAHPU team has used for tags.
{% /aside %}

## 8. Share the tag with your team

In `Settings` > `Documents` > `Print layouts`, export `Mammal voucher tags`.
The export includes the template its block uses, so colleagues receive both in
one file. A custom font is not included: install the same font on each device
that prints the tag, or choose a replacement when NAHPU asks during import.

## What you learned

- A template describes one tag; a print layout arranges tags on the sheet.
- `Insert Field` writes full `table::field` keys that fill from each record.
- `Text type` and `Format` turn stored values into printed dates and lists.
- `Conditional output` brackets imprecise measurements automatically.
- A QR code of the specimen UUID ties the physical tag to its record.
- Previewing with long and incomplete records catches layout problems before
  you print.

